# REGRESSION FIXTURE — B3: TWO SEQUENTIAL writes through the same
# `&`-projection. Both must land. Asserts 12; ggdef agrees.
#
# AXIS + CELL (Core #12): the WRITE-COUNT axis at the repeated-write cell — the
# only cell that separates a complete fix from one that resolves the place once
# and then re-reads a stale value.
#
# RED-VERIFIED: against the pre-fix compiler this printed 10 — both writes lost,
# same root as `sound_amp_byvalue_place_writethrough.gg`.
#
# GUARDED MECHANISM: the Family-1 chokepoint's per-call-site place resolution.
# Short-circuit `try_resolve_place` to `None` and this returns to 10.
#
# ⚠ WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE FIXTURE FROM THE SINGLE-WRITE ROW. A partial fix that
# write-throughs the FIRST projection and then re-reads a stale value (or
# materializes a fresh temp per call site) passes every single-write pin in this
# batch and prints 11 here. Only a second write to the same place catches it.
# The two calls are identical, so any per-site memoization of the projection is
# exercised.
#
# The seed is computed, not a literal.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (CoW / ownership / High): "By-value-field `&`-arg
# write-through is SILENTLY LOST" (repeated-write row).
struct Holder:
    int i

int seed():
    return 10

void bump_int(int &n):
    n = n + 1

void main():
    Holder h = Holder(seed())
    bump_int(&h.i)
    bump_int(&h.i)
    print(h.i)
